WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.260
All of us are moving towards
more virtual communication.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.034
That is a shift in context that's going to
change the way people understand you,

00:00:08.034 --> 00:00:09.640
the way people experience you.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.816
You cannot engage in exactly
the same behavior and

00:00:12.816 --> 00:00:17.814
assume that you're going to get exactly
the same results, the context shifts.

00:00:17.814 --> 00:00:22.868
&gt;&gt; It's been more than a year since we
went from spending time in meeting rooms

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:28.100
and long commutes, to meeting over
Zooms and accidentally staying on mute.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.607
As GSB Associate Dean Brian Lowry
just implied, we need to adjust our

00:00:32.607 --> 00:00:37.830
communication and presence to make
sure we get the results we want.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.572
My name is Matt Abrahams, and

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:44.620
I teach strategic communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:51.837
Welcome to this quick thinks episode
of Think Fast, Talk Smart, the podcast.

00:00:51.837 --> 00:00:56.345
In this episode, we're bringing
you some of the best advice we've

00:00:56.345 --> 00:01:01.249
received from guests on how to be more
successful in your professional and

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:04.432
personal life when
communicating virtually.

00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:09.644
Attention is the most valuable
commodity we have in the world today.

00:01:09.644 --> 00:01:13.091
More valuable than money,
more valuable than Bitcoin, and

00:01:13.091 --> 00:01:16.250
even more valuable than GameStop stock.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:20.618
When communicating virtually,
you need to work hard to both gain and

00:01:20.618 --> 00:01:22.667
maintain attention and focus.

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:26.238
It's all about being relevant and
engaging.

00:01:26.238 --> 00:01:31.254
Here are a few tips from
Professors Tina Seelig, Bob Sutton,

00:01:31.254 --> 00:01:35.700
and Sara Sual on how to keep
your audience interested.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:41.618
&gt;&gt; I think it's really important to
change up activities really quickly,

00:01:41.618 --> 00:01:43.743
like every 10 to 15 minutes.

00:01:43.743 --> 00:01:46.003
I almost feel it's almost
like Sesame Street.

00:01:46.003 --> 00:01:49.087
Every 10 to 15 minutes you need to change.

00:01:49.087 --> 00:01:53.765
Watching a video, to doing an activity,
to breaking into small groups.

00:01:53.765 --> 00:01:57.640
Folks get bored looking at
a screen if things are static, so

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.570
you need to plan a lot in advance.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.613
I view myself as more of a producer
when I'm planning an online class.

00:02:05.613 --> 00:02:09.753
There's a tremendous amount of thought
that goes into essentially scripting

00:02:09.753 --> 00:02:11.014
the whole experience.

00:02:11.014 --> 00:02:15.575
[MUSIC]

00:02:15.575 --> 00:02:18.850
&gt;&gt; I think we're all struggling
in this journey together.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.770
The first thing that I
would start out with, and

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:26.371
this is for those of us who are teachers,
who are executives, and also just

00:02:26.371 --> 00:02:31.267
having control of our calendar, is that
for emotional and cognitive reasons,

00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:35.674
we've got to pace ourselves and
encourage people to pace one another.

00:02:35.674 --> 00:02:37.853
So this means the meetings
have to be shorter.

00:02:37.853 --> 00:02:39.733
This means there has to be breaks.

00:02:39.733 --> 00:02:45.411
This means, and some of the folks at the
design school are really cool about this,

00:02:45.411 --> 00:02:49.052
consider doing 35% of
your meetings audio only.

00:02:49.052 --> 00:02:53.382
[MUSIC]

00:02:53.382 --> 00:02:59.117
&gt;&gt; So as educators, I think we have to
find ways to reduce cognitive overload.

00:02:59.117 --> 00:03:03.579
So, for example, I now offer my
information in much smaller pieces.

00:03:03.579 --> 00:03:07.999
Interpersed with breakout groups or
synchronous work and share documents,

00:03:07.999 --> 00:03:12.100
that's one of the tricks that
I've been experimenting with.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:16.056
I've also been working to make my
slides more visually appealing and

00:03:16.056 --> 00:03:17.740
much less cluttered.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.992
And this is where I've had the opportunity
to watch you teach frequently on slide

00:03:21.992 --> 00:03:24.836
design, and
this has been very helpful to me.

00:03:24.836 --> 00:03:25.743
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you.

00:03:25.743 --> 00:03:30.130
Presence is also critical to success
when communicating virtually.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:33.615
We need to be able to authentically
connect to our audiences,

00:03:33.615 --> 00:03:38.450
not just through our words, but
also through our nonverbal behaviors.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.682
Here are my favorite quick
tips from Carol Robbins,

00:03:41.682 --> 00:03:47.150
Professors Christian Wheeler and Jennifer
Aaker, and lecturer, Naomi Bagdonis.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:50.904
On how simple and
slight shifts in your presence can

00:03:50.904 --> 00:03:55.802
help you connect more authentically
with your remote audiences.

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:59.305
&gt;&gt; The fact is that I
think we've become much

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:03.980
more transactional as
a result of the pandemic.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:10.651
And much more task-oriented, especially in
business, at the expense of relationships.

00:04:10.651 --> 00:04:15.739
So we have a lot of contact, maybe,
but not a whole lot of connection.

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:20.699
And if we want more connection, then
we've gotta make a little bit of time to

00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:23.499
ask each other, how are you really doing?

00:04:23.499 --> 00:04:27.538
[MUSIC]

00:04:27.538 --> 00:04:32.094
&gt;&gt; One thing that organizations need to
think about is ways to artificially induce

00:04:32.094 --> 00:04:36.385
that into this more formal Zoom-style
interaction that we've adopted.

00:04:36.385 --> 00:04:41.045
So in my exercises, what we do is we have
people engage in exercises where they're

00:04:41.045 --> 00:04:42.880
interacting with one another.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.920
They're experiencing this positive ethic,
and they're engaging in some degree of

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.749
self-disclosure, which is something
that creates the foundations of trust.

00:04:50.749 --> 00:04:53.749
[MUSIC]

00:04:53.749 --> 00:04:58.436
&gt;&gt; It used to be that leaders
needed to be revered, and

00:04:58.436 --> 00:05:01.388
now they need to be understood.

00:05:01.388 --> 00:05:07.346
And all the while, humor is
a particularly potent elixir for trust.

00:05:07.346 --> 00:05:12.052
And when we laugh with someone, be it in
person or even over screens through Zoom.

00:05:12.052 --> 00:05:16.852
The bottom line, Matt,
is giving birth, having sex, and

00:05:16.852 --> 00:05:22.930
laughing with colleagues in Zoom
meetings actually have a lot in common.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.468
We're building trust and
no one's wearing pants.

00:05:25.468 --> 00:05:27.828
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] There you go.
[MUSIC]

00:05:27.828 --> 00:05:31.654
Technology has allowed us to
connect like never before, as so

00:05:31.654 --> 00:05:33.880
many of us work from home.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:38.379
But as we all know, technology can really
make or break how we're perceived and

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:40.870
how we get our messages across.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:45.600
Use these straightforward tips
that I share with my students

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.430
when you use Zoom, Teams, Meet,
or any other virtual tool.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:53.362
How you show up really matters.

00:05:53.362 --> 00:05:58.170
Make sure you fill up half the screen
with your face and upper body.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:02.841
Make sure there's a little bit of room
between your head and the top of your box.

00:06:02.841 --> 00:06:06.690
Check this out before you
actually join the meeting.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.720
You have to be well lit, too.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.448
As human beings,
we read a lot on people's faces.

00:06:12.448 --> 00:06:16.305
And if we can't see your face, if you're
covered in the shadows like you're in

00:06:16.305 --> 00:06:19.717
the witness protection program,
it makes it hard for us to trust you.

00:06:19.717 --> 00:06:23.040
Be well lit and fill up the screen.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.090
You also need to make sure you raise your
camera up so it's right at your eye level.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:31.957
So it looks like we're looking directly
at you when you're speaking to us.

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:34.229
And make sure you look at that camera so

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:37.792
it looks like you're looking
at us as we're watching you.

00:06:37.792 --> 00:06:42.470
Making eye contact is critical for
building connection and trust.

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:46.008
I take a picture of my family,
who on most days I like,

00:06:46.008 --> 00:06:48.461
and I tape it right behind my camera.

00:06:48.461 --> 00:06:52.097
So I'm looking at people I
care about as I present.

00:06:52.097 --> 00:06:56.610
That draws my attention to the camera,
and can be really helpful for connecting.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.472
Variation is also critical in your voice.

00:07:00.472 --> 00:07:06.190
If people are monotone or their rate stays
the same, we lose focus and attention.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.242
We're designed to pay attention to
things that change and vary, so

00:07:10.242 --> 00:07:12.470
make sure you vary your voice.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:17.364
A great way to do this is to add emotive
words, adjectives, adverbs, descriptors.

00:07:17.364 --> 00:07:21.846
We tend to inflect our
voice as we say those.

00:07:21.846 --> 00:07:25.006
Make sure also that you gesture.

00:07:25.006 --> 00:07:27.135
Now, gestures are different
when you're virtual.

00:07:27.135 --> 00:07:31.165
You need to make sure your hands
are up and visible in the box.

00:07:31.165 --> 00:07:34.324
That means keeping your thumbs
about at your shoulder height.

00:07:34.324 --> 00:07:38.415
And always make sure to gesture
slightly beyond your shoulders, not so

00:07:38.415 --> 00:07:40.200
far that you go out of your box.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.806
But you don't want your gestures
in front of your face or

00:07:43.806 --> 00:07:46.885
your chest,
it makes you look tense and tight.

00:07:46.885 --> 00:07:51.749
Finally, when managing interaction,
like conversation during a meeting or

00:07:51.749 --> 00:07:55.305
during a Q&amp;A session,
leverage paraphrasing.

00:07:55.305 --> 00:07:58.695
Paraphrasing is a tool that helps
you signal that you've heard what

00:07:58.695 --> 00:08:00.065
somebody said.

00:08:00.065 --> 00:08:04.265
But it also allows you to interrupt and
interject, simply highlight or

00:08:04.265 --> 00:08:06.725
summarize something somebody has said.

00:08:06.725 --> 00:08:10.585
And then add your opinion and
link or bridge to the next topic.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:17.203
Taken together, working on engagement,
presence, and how you can maximize

00:08:17.203 --> 00:08:22.692
your remote tools set up, you can enhance
and hone your virtual communication.

00:08:22.692 --> 00:08:26.367
Taking the time to work on these
skills will serve you well.

00:08:26.367 --> 00:08:31.580
Because while we're all excited to return
to more in-person communication and

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.650
interaction, virtual communication
is likely here to stay.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:43.018
Thanks for joining us for another episode
of Think Fast, Talk Smart, the podcast.

